    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d66">

                <pb facs="0067" n="61" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg61"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">856B.20/34: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Venezuela</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Corrigan</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-26T18:00:00-04:30">January 26, 1942—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:16 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>49. Department’s No. 55[bis] January 24, 9 p.m. I talked with the President last
                    night and again this morning. Appreciating the essential motivating factors of
                    time, secrecy and speed, he has no objection to the defense measures already
                    taken but emphatically affirms Venezuela’s primary interest in the matter as
                    expressed in the note of July 3, 1940.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d66-f1"/> He
                    informed me that a definition of Venezuela’s position and participation will be
                    formulated at once and made clear in an official communication.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Amdelgat, Rio, for Under Secretary.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Corrigan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
